# Security headers for the Quotely web app on Cloudflare Pages.
#
# The app holds a JWT and displays financial documents, so the cheap browser-side protections are
# worth having.
#
# Two of the values below are load-bearing and easy to get wrong:
#
# Permissions-Policy — payment= is delegated to Razorpay Checkout ON PURPOSE. Checkout runs in an
# iframe and uses the Payment Request API for Google Pay and card autofill. A bare payment=()
# disables that API for the page AND everything embedded in it, so Checkout still opens, still
# looks correct, and simply cannot offer Google Pay. Nothing throws, so the first sign of trouble
# is a customer who could not pay. Every other capability stays denied: this app has no use for a
# camera, a microphone or a location, so nothing it embeds should be able to ask for one.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security — now that Quotely is served from a domain we own, and every host
# under it is HTTPS-only (Cloudflare Pages for the site, Render for api.), committing to HTTPS is
# safe. It was not while the app lived on a shared *.pages.dev. One year, and includeSubDomains so
# api.quotely4you.org is covered too. Deliberately NOT preloaded: the preload list is baked into
# browser binaries and removal takes months, which is not a commitment worth making for a product
# this young.
#
# Comments live here rather than inside the rule block below, because the _headers format treats
# every indented line in a block as a header.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(self "https://checkout.razorpay.com" "https://api.razorpay.com")
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
